date-released: 2026-11-11
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Version 0.3.0 adds MAG-S3c, a pre-registered simulation study of whether the conclusions of MAG-S3b survive the
  real geometry of the radical pair in cryptochrome 4 and spin relaxation. It recomputes the three-nucleus pair in
  the FAD–W_C and FAD–W_D pairs of pigeon cryptochrome 4 (PDB 6PU0), with their dipolar coupling, with singlet-triplet
  dephasing up to 1e8 and random fields up to 3e7 per second. Its protocol, code and notebook were frozen and their
  SHA-256 posted in Telegram no later than the minute the run started; the run was continued in parts of 1.5 h after a flaw of the pause, a deviation
  recorded before the continuation. All eleven invariants held and a reanalysis reproduced every value. The odd
  dipole follows the hyperfine tensors, the singlet-triplet coherence creates it, it falls by half at a dephasing
  rate of 3.3–6.2e7 per second (above 1e8 for one axis), inside the range measured in Cry4a or above it, and the dipole test sees both candidate pairs.
  The dominance of the coherence form of CISS over the polarisation forms holds only without the dipolar coupling.
  Version 0.2.0 added MAG-S3b to MAG-S3. MAG-S3b is a pre-registered simulation study of whether the odd part of a
  radical-pair (cryptochrome 4) compass code can carry the sign of the geomagnetic field to neurons through
  chirality-induced spin selectivity (CISS), how the polar and axial order of cryptochromes gates it, and whether a
  blind estimate recovers its size from a MAG-S3 recording. Its protocol, code and notebook were frozen and their
  SHA-256 posted before the run was read; the run started three minutes before the freeze on the same files, a
  deviation recorded with the release. All invariants held. The coherence form of CISS gave 253 times more odd part
  than the polarisation forms, the blind estimate was calibrated, and a dipole test found the odd part at an
  effective admixture five times below the antipodal test of MAG-S3. The odd part stayed below the antipodal
  threshold in the reference design, departed from proportionality at the largest CISS angle, and did not follow
  the cos beta law, because the hyperfine tensors set the direction of the odd dipole together with the CISS axis.
  MAG-S3 itself, unchanged since version 0.1.1, is a simulation study of the recording experiment that could test
  whether neurons reading a radical-pair compass carry a population code shaped like the real projective plane RP2.
  Version 0.2.1 adds notes after release to MAG-S3b: exact consequences of its reciprocity criterion, one of which
  limits the reading of the odd part as a trace of CISS to pairs that recombine spin-selectively where they were born,
  and works found by a source check; the frozen files, the runs and the verdicts are those of version 0.2.0.
  Code is released under MIT; text, the protocols and the results under CC BY 4.0.
keywords:
